            "text": "Makes a tensor of random 0s and 1s: each element is 1 with the probability you set, otherwise 0.\n\nEach element is an independent biased coin flip. Use it for random masks (which cells to keep, which to drop), random triggers, sparse patterns of dots, or a yes/no decision whose odds you control.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dist.bernoulli <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nWhole numbers give the shape of each batch of samples; with none\nthe node sends one sample at a time.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.dist.bernoulli 64 64\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a fresh tensor of samples. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe size of the tensor of samples, as whole numbers: [64, 64] is a 64 by 64 grid, [1000] a row of a thousand. Numbers typed after the node name set it (t.dist.bernoulli 64 64); with none it is [1], a single sample. A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send by itself - the next click uses it.\n\nprobability (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe chance that each element is 1, from 0 (all zeros) to 1 (all ones). The drag float stays within 0..1; a number outside it sent to the inlet is refused (a message in the console) and the node keeps its last probability. Default 0.5.\n\nTHE logits FORM:\nt.dist.bernoulli logits (any word after the name other than probs) replaces the probability with a 'logits' parameter, default 1: the probability is then 1 / (1 + e to the power -logits), so 0 means even odds, large positive values nearly always 1, large negative nearly always 0. Useful when the odds come out of a neural network.\n\nEach parameter is a drag float on the node and also an inlet. Setting one rebuilds the distribution but does NOT send; the next click samples from it. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging a slider re-samples at once. A parameter that must be above 0 has a drag float that stops at 0.000001. A value the distribution cannot take (see each parameter) - 0 or below sent into such an inlet, say - prints a message in the console, and the node goes on sampling with the last parameters that worked.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nA float32 tensor on the cpu, in the shape set, filled with fresh independent samples on every click.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang draws the first 4096 samples when the page opens; click the node's own button for more. The heat_map shows the coin flips: 1 white, 0 black. At 0.5 it is salt and pepper; drag probability towards 0 and the white dots thin out, towards 1 the black ones do. The histogram has just two bars - the count of 0s on the left, of 1s on the right - and t.mean of the samples is the fraction that came up 1, close to the probability you set.\n\nRELATED:\nt.bernoulli makes a 0/1 flip for every element of a tensor of probabilities - a different probability for each element. t.dist.continuous_bernoulli is a smooth 0..1 cousin. t.rand compared with a threshold does the same job by hand."
        },
